Build: Simplified AI Visibility module (v2), API-based, one-click

Goal: Add a lighter, faster version of the existing AI Visibility module that gets LLM responses via API calls instead of the current method, with a fully automated one-click flow.

Flow:

User adds a domain and saves the project.
System auto-identifies the business, its products/services (from the domain — scrape/fetch homepage + key pages, or use an LLM call on the domain content).
System auto-generates 10 prompts based on that business/product/service identification — no manual prompt writing needed for these.
Each of the 10 prompts is sent to the relevant AI APIs to get responses.
From those responses, compute the same metrics the current AI Visibility app already produces (reuse existing metric logic/functions — do not reinvent).
Output should be usable in the same UI/format patterns as the current module where possible.

Prompt management:

User can add up to 10 additional custom prompts on top of the 10 auto-generated ones (max 20 total per project).
User can delete any prompt (auto-generated or custom) and add new ones, up to the 20 cap.
Auto-generated and user-added prompts are treated identically once created — same edit/delete/run behavior.

Model/API to use (query these for each prompt run):

OpenAI — Responses API, model gpt-5-nano (cheapest OpenAI model that supports the web_search tool). Enable web_search.
Anthropic — Messages API, model claude-haiku-4-5-20251001 (cheapest current Claude model with web_search tool support). Enable web_search.
Google — Gemini API, model gemini-2.5-flash-lite (cheapest Gemini model, supports Grounding with Google Search). Enable the google_search grounding tool — without it the model answers from training data only, not real search.

Verify these model names/pricing against each provider's live pricing page at build time — cheapest-tier models shift.

How to call the APIs (mimic a real user search):

Natural conversational phrasing for prompts, no system-prompt bias, default/moderate temperature.
Capture both response text and any citations/sources array returned by each API — both feed into metric calculations (text mention vs. citation mention are separate signals).

